Z||000000||superXmotorsports||11-14-1999||10:23 AM||||WKDFIRO commented about the dual friction Centerforce in the this is what's in my 3.2 thread.  Anyone else have an opinion on what the best clutch to use with a hi-po rebuild 3.2?  I was just about to order my d/f when I read that reply.  Thanks!!!!||||reg||2
Z||000001||batboy||11-14-1999||01:57 PM||||I read that post and it's the first negative thing I've heard about Centerforce clutches. A friend of a friend races SCCA events with a Vette. He was going through a clutch every couple years and tried various brands until he switched to Centerforce several years ago and has not had any problems since. I don't know if he uses a dual friction disk or the standard disk though.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Eric||11-14-1999||05:57 PM||||I think he's trying to sell his own clutch.  I'm sure there's nothing wrong with the Centerforce.  The company has the best reputation of all the clutch manufacturers.  As far as the dual friction goes, I don't think a 3.2 rebuild really needs it.  The standard centerforce clutch should suffice, but in my case, the dual friction was only about fifty bucks more.  So it may, in fact, be overkill.||||reg||1
Z||000003||superXmotorsports||11-14-1999||06:28 PM||||Eric - how much did you pay for the dual-friction?  My cost is $227.00.||||reg||1
Z||000004||JIM GOOSE||11-17-1999||08:34 AM||||I have the CF dual-friction(orange cover)in my car & has worked good for 5k miles. These are street miles not drag strip so havent been abusive to it. Its has a light feeling as far as your left leg is concerned but engages in a positive manner. One thing thats missing on the FIERO pressureplate is the funny little weights shown in Centerforce ads that are supposed to add grip at high rpm. Not room for these due to its small size. Anyway, my experience has been positive, so far.||||reg||7
Z||000005||Carrolles||11-17-1999||12:42 PM||||Exactly what is meant by "Dual Friction"?  <BR>Every clutch I've ever seen has friction material on both sides of the disk.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||88 Fiero||11-17-1999||01:09 PM||||In response to your comments about WKDFIRO...First of all he is the President of Southern Cal's "Fieros West". He is a very avid fan of the Fiero and has been keen involve with repairs and performance with the Fiero... He also help work on some cars for "Fiero Motorsports" in Anaheim,CA... Keith who is the owner of "Fiero Motorsports" is a GM graduate mechanic and has established is reputation as the "Fiero Doc"... He has installed many V8 engines on a Fiero whether they are carburated or fuel injected... as well as modifying the 2.8 or punching it out to 3.1 liters and turbo charging them... The issue with Centerforce is that they found out that they are not as good as they use to be or there might be a company selling clutches and just paying Center Force to use their name. WKDFIRO doesn't own a shop or sells accessories for the Fiero. He got a V6 on his Fiero which the engine is also modified. I know him for awhile and I can atest you to his knowledge of the Fiero. Like I said before...FOCOA Club was born before the Fiero was release and it was the first Fiero Club that was ever formed... Keith being the son of Phil owner of "FOCOA"...has been extensively involve with this cars...they even travel and met the people who designed and manufacture this car at the Pontiac Assembly Plant in the early '80's... Also some of the events that FOCOA did in the past is blast our car thru the Indianapolis Motor Speedway and that was fun...So rest assure that this is a shared info that they concluded after testing this product so many times...In other words, what he wants to say is that to be careful what you buy...and be sure you pay for what's your moneys worth.....||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phil||11-17-1999||02:27 PM||||Centerforce makes/sells at least 3 different pressure plate assemblys. Their normal duty street plate doesn't have the weights the street/strip and full competition ones do have weights. Also 2 types of clutch disks  normal (same material on both sides) and dual friction (flywheel side is some reddish-brown stuff and the other side is a something and bronze). I have a McCloud plate with a dual friction disk.||||reg||1
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||11-17-1999||02:56 PM||||Why thank you 88GT for the vote of confidence.<P>I needed to correct what I said earlier. The preasure plate to avoid is LUK not DUK. What we were told is that the Fiero trany is to cramped for the balancing weights so they outsource this LUK preasure plate. We have seen this plate painted orange as well as nonpainted coming from the local clutch shop. Thanks once again 88, in agreeing with me in warning all of you to be careful with what you pay your hard earned money for!||||reg||1
Z||000009||WKDFIRO||11-17-1999||03:16 PM||||Oh, one more thing....<P>The suggestions and views that I offer are by no means meant to be implied as law. They are only meant to help. There is so much misinformation from non Fiero people its frightening. As for CenterForce, I am not saying that ALL of their clutches are bad, just that some of their preasure plates have been known to do very bad things, but not every single one of them. If you have one in your car and you have no problem with it than have a good time. Many people say that Fieros in general, suck. We all know that there are basketcase, moneyhole Fieros and Fieros that last forever. I just don't want to come off as someone who thinks they know everything there is to know about Fieros. Keith (Fiero Doc) Huff and John Parenti of Fiero Motorsports know WAY more than I do. Many people go to them because of it. Im just trying to help by offering what I have seen. I love this place for the exchange of ideas!||||reg||1
Z||000010||superXmotorsports||11-17-1999||08:51 PM||||I respect your expertise and I am a FOCOA member but what clutch DO you recommend or use (this will be for a maxed out 3.2 stroker)  Thanks!||||reg||4
