Z||000013||Phil||11-19-1999||06:26 AM||||Like I said above I'm running a McCloud with my 3800 and so far no problems, but it is a tight fit.||||reg||1
Z||000014||2birds||11-20-1999||11:42 PM||||I'm new here and I have a stock-engined 86 5-speed GT (crossed 162,000 miles today!).  WKDFIRO stated earlier that the LUK pressure plate should be avoided.  Why is this?  I had my clutch replaced by a dealer back in 1990 and they used one with LUK stamped on it.  When I did the clutch myself back in 96 I ordered a kit from RAM, via Summit.  The RAM people gave me a special disk with 6 springs instead of the 4 in the original one, but the pressure plate was identical to the one the dealer used.  I've put 40,000 miles on it since then, and I've never been easy on it, and I haven't had any problems.  ||||reg||1
Z||000015||superXmotorsports||11-21-1999||10:14 AM||||I just re-read an old article in my FOCOA magazine saying: "The Centerforce clutch is not made by Centerforce.  It does not have any Centerforce weights as advertised.  It is a Japanese Daiken clutch painted Centerforce orange.  The partial solution is using a bigger clutch..."  So I am now stumped.  The Centerforce dual-friction seemed like the ultimate clutch.  I have had clutch problems in more than 1/2 of the 20 or so Fieros I have owned.  They claim their 9 3/4" with 6 springs and optional materials is the best, however it very tough to reach someone for more detail and this clutch will cost me 3X what the d/f would.||||reg||1
Z||000016||mwbackus||11-21-1999||05:37 PM||||Justin and his crew (ie..the Fiero Store) did alot of research into the best clutch to use on the Fiero, and they came up with what many believe is the ideal solution.  <P>    One has to keep in mind that the 2.8 V6 is not a high reving engine, and the centrifical forces used by the center force clutch (weights, etc..) to enhance clamping pressures are realized at higher rpms (torque is actully quite low on the 2.8 at these rpms).  <BR>But, the Fiero V6 does have a considerable amount of torque at low rpms, with alot of weight directly over the wheels, and very little drivetrain to absorb this abrupt power.  This is the primary cause of excessive disk slippage on the Fiero clutch, and the reason for its premature wear.  <P>     The best clutch for this situation is a heavy duty plate, that incorporates a kevlar disk to effectivly limit this wear and give a good low rpm clamping force.  The Fiero Store offers this type of system, as does the well known Fiero enthusuast group "West Coast Fiero's".  No need to recreate the wheel here guys, the research and development has already been done.<P>Mike||||reg||1
